---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
import CodeGroup from "../../components/CodeGroup.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Concepts</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    The few ideas the whole library is built on: cascade layers, light and dark,
    how props map to classes, rich text, and when you need JavaScript.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="cascade-layers">Cascade layers</h2>
    <p>
      Every rule in OPUI lives in a cascade layer. <code>layers.css</code> declares
      the order once, and everything after it slots into place:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer openprops, theme, normalize, components.prose, components.root, components.extended, utils;`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>openprops</code>: the Open Props tokens (<code>--size-3</code>,
        <code>--font-size-1</code>, …).
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>theme</code>: <code>palette.css</code> and <code>theme.css</code>,
        the colors and component tokens like <code>--primary</code> and <code
          >--button-size</code
        >.
      </li>
      <li><code>normalize</code>: the reset.</li>
      <li>
        <code>components.prose</code>: rich text, lowest of the components so a
        component inside rich text keeps its own styles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>components.root</code>: components that stand on their own, like
        Button and Checkbox.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>components.extended</code>: components built on top of others,
        like Badge, Dialog and Select.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>utils</code>: utility classes such as <code>.ui-sr-only</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A later layer always beats an earlier one, no matter the specificity. And
      anything you write outside a layer beats all of them. So you never have to
      fight the library with <code>!important</code> or long selectors:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`/* Unlayered, so it wins over every OPUI layer */
.ui-card {
  border-radius: 0;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      Import <code>layers.css</code> first when you import single files, so the order
      is set before anything lands in a layer. <DocLink
        href="/guide/customizing">Customizing</DocLink
      > has more on where to put your own styles.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="light-and-dark">Light and dark</h2>
    <p>
      OPUI follows the OS setting out of the box: <code>theme.css</code> sets <code
        >color-scheme: light dark</code
      > and the color tokens use <code>light-dark()</code>, so there is no dark
      mode class to toggle. By default the palette is green in light mode and
      blue in dark mode.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>.ui-light</code> and <code>.ui-dark</code> force a scheme. Put them on
      <code>html</code> for the whole page, or on any element to force just that part.
    </p>
    <Example>
      <div class="concepts-schemes">
        <div class="ui-light">
          <button class="ui-button ui-filled ui-primary" type="button">
            Always light
          </button>
        </div>
        <div class="ui-dark">
          <button class="ui-button ui-filled ui-primary" type="button">
            Always dark
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Example>
    <p>
      More on scopes and the palette in <DocLink
        href="/guide/getting-started#theming">Theming</DocLink
      >.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="props-and-classes">Props and classes</h2>
    <p>
      Every class the library owns starts with <code>ui-</code>, so it never
      clashes with yours. A component is one class on the right element, and its
      options are modifier classes next to it. The Astro and Vue components take
      the same options as props and render those exact classes, so the HTML and
      the props map one to one:
    </p>
    <CodeGroup
      tabs={[
        {
          title: "HTML",
          code: `<button class="ui-button ui-outlined ui-small ui-primary" type="button">
  Save
</button>`,
          lang: "html",
        },
        {
          title: "Astro",
          code: `<Button variant="outlined" size="small" color="primary">Save</Button>`,
          lang: "astro",
        },
        {
          title: "Vue",
          code: `<Button variant="outlined" size="small" color="primary">Save</Button>`,
          lang: "vue",
        },
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      Boolean props turn into a class of the same name (<code>rounded</code> is <code
        >.ui-rounded</code
      >), and native states stay native: a disabled button is <code
        >disabled</code
      >, not a class. Every component's API table lists the prop and the class
      side by side, so switching between frameworks (or dropping down to plain
      HTML) is mostly copy and paste.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="rich-text">Rich text</h2>
    <p>
      Content you don't control, like Markdown or CMS output, has no classes.
      Wrap it in <code>.ui-rich-text</code> and the plain elements inside get styled:
      headings, paragraphs, lists, links, tables, quotes and code. Only elements without
      a class are touched, so components inside rich text look the same as anywhere
      else.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>.ui-not-rich-text</code> stops it for a subtree, handy for a demo or a
      widget in the middle of an article.
    </p>
    <Example richText="not-rich-text">
      <article class="ui-rich-text">
        <h3>Release notes</h3>
        <p>Plain HTML, styled by the wrapper.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>No classes needed</li>
          <li>Components keep their own styles</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="ui-not-rich-text">
          <ul>
            <li>This list is left alone</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>
    </Example>
    <p>
      Need a heading style outside rich text, or on a heading that has a class?
      Use <code>.ui-h1</code>–<code>.ui-h6</code>. More on the <DocLink
        href="/components/typography">Typography</DocLink
      > page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="javascript">JavaScript</h2>
    <p>
      OPUI is CSS first, and the HTML platform does a lot these days. These work
      without a single line of JavaScript:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Accordion is a <code>details</code> element, Tabs are a radio group, and ToggleButton
        and ToggleGroup are checkboxes and radios.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dialog, Drawer and Menu open with Invoker Commands (<code>command</code> and
        <code>commandfor</code> on a button) and the Popover API.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tooltip and hover Anchor show with <code>interestfor</code> and CSS anchor
        positioning.
      </li>
      <li>
        Carousel buttons and markers are generated by CSS, and the Range track
        fill is a scroll-driven animation.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Some of these are fresh off the press, so check <DocLink
        href="/guide/browser-support">browser support</DocLink
      > for what falls back and where.
    </p>
    <p>A few things do need script in plain HTML:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Toast</strong>: <code>css/js/toast.js</code> has <code
          >initToastManager()</code
        > and <code>showToast()</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Indeterminate checkboxes</strong>: <code>indeterminate</code> is a
        property, not an attribute. Mark the input with <code
          >data-indeterminate</code
        > and call <code>activateIndeterminate()</code> from <code
          >css/js/checkbox.js</code
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Range value</strong>: the <code>output</code> that shows the current
        value has to be updated on <code>input</code>. The Astro component ships
        a tiny script for it, and Vue does it reactively.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      code={`<script type="module">
  import { activateIndeterminate } from "opui-css/css/js/checkbox.js"
  import { initToastManager } from "opui-css/css/js/toast.js"

  activateIndeterminate()
  initToastManager()
</script>`}
      lang="html"
    />
  </section>
</Guide>

<style>
  .concepts-schemes {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--size-3);

    > div {
      border-radius: var(--border-radius);
      padding: var(--size-4);
    }
  }
</style>
